# IMPULSE — push, launch or pull, spread over several ticks so a child can SEE it happen.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT ONE BIG SHOVE. A single large Motion set moves the victim the whole distance in
# one tick. There is no motion to watch: you were here, now you are there, and a seven-year-old
# reads that as the game glitching rather than as the monster hitting them. Real games ramp it, and
# the difference between "it teleported me" and "it PUNCHED me" is entirely in those few frames.
#
# So the movement is applied as a SLICE PER TICK for RAMP_TICKS ticks, counted down on the victim
# itself. That needs per-victim state, which is why impulse earns a template instead of riding the
# COMMAND parameter like every other effect so far.
#
# MOVE and VICTIM_SELECTOR are built by apply_effect from friendly words (direction, strength,
# feel, target), because the child says "so hard i fly backwards" and not "0.8 blocks per tick".
#
# THE SOURCE CARRIES ITS OWN CLOCK (mfam_impt) so "every 5 seconds" is a parameter rather than a
# different template. With PERIOD_TICKS=1 it fires whenever the selector matches, which is what
# on_hit and proximity want; with PERIOD_TICKS=100 it is a stomp every five seconds. Tier 3.5 found
# this gap by reading the description: impulse offered no timed trigger at all, so "Spikor knocks
# everyone back every 5 seconds" collapsed to a continuous proximity push.
reset
    /scoreboard objectives add mfam_imp dummy
    /scoreboard objectives add mfam_impt dummy
    /scoreboard objectives add mfam_noop dummy
end

clock tick
    # 1. the source's own clock. PERIOD_TICKS=1 means "as often as the trigger allows".
    /execute as @e[tag=${TAG}] run scoreboard players add @s mfam_impt 1
    # 2. when the clock is up, whoever the trigger catches starts a countdown
    /execute as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] at @s run execute as ${VICTIM_SELECTOR} run scoreboard players set @s mfam_imp ${RAMP_TICKS}
    # 3. TELEGRAPH: something has to happen where the push starts, or the movement has no cause
    /execute unless entity @e[tag=${TAG},tag=mfam_veiled,limit=1] as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] at @s run particle ${PARTICLE} ~ ~0.2 ~ ${RADIUS} 0.2 ${RADIUS} 0.06 60 force
    /execute if entity @e[tag=${TAG},tag=mfam_veiled,limit=1] as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] at @s run particle ${PARTICLE} ~ ~0.2 ~ ${RADIUS} 0.2 ${RADIUS} 0.06 60 force @a[tag=!mfam_veil_blind]
    /execute unless entity @e[tag=${TAG},tag=mfam_veiled,limit=1] as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] at @s run playsound ${SOUND} master @a ~ ~ ~ 1 1
    /execute if entity @e[tag=${TAG},tag=mfam_veiled,limit=1] as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] at @s run playsound ${SOUND} master @a[tag=!mfam_veil_blind] ~ ~ ~ 1 1
    /execute as @e[tag=${TAG},scores={mfam_impt=${PERIOD_TICKS}..}] run scoreboard players set @s mfam_impt 0
    # 4. LANDING: a big upward launch is lethal on the way down, which is not what anybody wished
    #    for. For up-launches this hands out slow falling; otherwise it is a no-op.
    /execute as @e[scores={mfam_imp=${RAMP_TICKS}}] at @s run ${LANDING}
    # 5. one slice of the movement per tick, so it READS as a shove rather than a teleport
    /execute as @e[scores={mfam_imp=1..}] at @s run ${MOVE}
    /execute as @e[scores={mfam_imp=1..}] run scoreboard players remove @s mfam_imp 1
end
